## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of stability and permutation-based sorting, ensuring candidates can separate data from its keys and reason about time/space trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sort
- Permutations
- Time/space complexity
- Merge-based sorting on indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableSortIndicesByValue(A){
  const idx = Array.from({length: A.length}, (_, i) => i);
  function merge(left, right){
    const res = [];
    let i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < left.length && j < right.length){
      if (A[left[i]] <= A[right[j]]) res.push(left[i++]);
      else res.push(right[j++]);
    }
    while (i < left.length) res.push(left[i++]);
    while (j < right.length) res.push(right[j++]);
    return res;
  }
  function sort(arr){
    if (arr.length <= 1) return arr;
    const m = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
    const L = sort(arr.slice(0, m));
    const R = sort(arr.slice(m));
    return merge(L, R);
  }
  const P = sort(idx);
  return { P, sorted: P.map(i => A[i]) };
}
```

## Interview Context
This question tests distributed processing, memory management, and algorithmic optimization skills for senior engineering roles dealing with big data scenarios.

## Technical Approach
- **Memory Mapping**: Use `mmap` for zero-copy file access, avoiding data duplication
- **Streaming Algorithm**: Process file in fixed-size chunks (64KB-1MB) with bounded memory
- **Data Structure**: Min-heap (O(log n)) for top-10 maintenance vs full sort (O(n log n))
- **Parallel Processing**: Split file into N chunks, process concurrently, then merge results

## Go Implementation
```go
type IPCounter struct {
    counts map[string]int
    heap   *MinHeap
    mu     sync.RWMutex
}

func (ic *IPCounter) processChunk(chunk []byte) {
    scanner := bufio.NewScanner(bytes.NewReader(chunk))
    for scanner.Scan() {
        ip := extractIP(scanner.Text())
        ic.mu.Lock()
        ic.counts[ip]++
        ic.mu.Unlock()
    }
}

func (ic *IPCounter) getTop10() []IPCount {
    heap := container.NewMinHeap(10)
    for ip, count := range ic.counts {
        if heap.Len() < 10 {
            heap.Push(IPCount{ip, count})
        } else if count > heap.Peek().Count {
            heap.Pop()
            heap.Push(IPCount{ip, count})
        }
    }
    return heap.ToSlice()
}
```

